              I'm <strong>S. Khavin</strong>, a final-year Computer Science Engineering student at
              Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology, Chennai. My instinct to inspect how
              systems compute began around age seven building redstone and command-block circuits
              in Minecraft—taking mechanisms apart before trying to build something cleaner.
            </p>
            <p>
              Today my research examines the internal computational structure of modern neural
              networks. Using causal interventions, representation geometry, and
              neuroscience-inspired ablations, I study how language and vision-language models
              encode tasks, transform residual activations, and route long-range context.
            </p>
            <p>
              That inquiry emerged from practical bottlenecks while building <em>AgentFox</em> (a
              local agentic browser) and <em>Trinetra</em>: even when a language model only ingests
              selected video frames, quadratic attention still dominates cost. Standard inference
              allocates the same compute evaluating <code>3 + 7</code> as synthesizing production
              code.
            </p>
            <p>
